The workflow inserts git commit --no-edit between git add -u and git
rebase --continue because git notes can only annotate commits that
already exist — you can't attach a note to a commit and then create
it in the same step. The rebase doesn't require this split; it's
purely to make room for the annotation before --continue moves past
the commit.

The /tmp/auto-merge.diff path is a placeholder — in practice, each
session should use a session-specific path (e.g. /tmp/rebase/<branch>/)
so parallel sessions don't clobber each other. Left generic here
since the skill doesn't know the branch name ahead of time.

An alternative would be storing the notes inline with -m only, skipping
the -F file. That loses the full diff, which is the most useful part
for forensic replay. The -F approach is slightly more ceremony but
meaningfully more informative.
